  {"clause_id": "c001",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The definition of the employee party is garbled (\"You,\" \"you,\" \"your,\" or \"Fy\"), so the shorthand for the counterparty is left incomplete on the face of the text. The recital also purports to bind \"your heirs and successors\" without any operative provision giving them rights.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20},
     {"issue": "The introductory clause binds \"your heirs and successors\" while Section 8 (Successors) makes the Agreement binding only upon successors and assigns of the Company, an asymmetry a court could be asked to reconcile.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Company may cut the Retention Period short if it \"determines\" the \"services are substantially complete,\" but \"services\" is never defined anywhere in the Agreement and \"substantially complete\" has no benchmark. Whether that determination is unreviewable or subject to good faith is genuinely contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42},
     {"issue": "The proviso that the Agreement continues \"provided that the Agreement or services are not otherwise terminated in accordance with any other provision\" sits uneasily with Section 12, which lets the Company terminate at any time but forbids any termination that diminishes accrued rights. The two provisions give different answers to what an early Company termination does to the Payment.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The promise of $60,300 is made \"subject to the terms and conditions of this Agreement, including any applicable adjustment, forfeiture and adjusted time of payment provisions,\" a catch-all that does not identify which provisions those are. Its reach against the specific forfeiture triggers in Section 2(c) and Section 14 is open to argument.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
     {"issue": "The unconditional payment obligation must be squared with Section 12's power to terminate the Agreement at any time and with Section 14's freestanding forfeiture for disclosure, which are not cross-referenced here. A court could be asked whether the Payment vested or remained wholly defeasible.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The provision turns on whether \"you are on an assignment under IBAS terms,\" but the acronym IBAS is never defined or incorporated anywhere in the instrument. The garbled opening words compound the uncertainty about what the employee is responsible for.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.33},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.08}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"The Payment may be affected by a change in your duties or position as set forth below\" is indeterminate about whether \"affected\" means only the proration in Section 2(a) or something broader. The permissive \"may\" leaves the consequence of a position change unfixed.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
     {"issue": "This at-will statement, read with Section 4's power to terminate with or without cause, is in tension with Section 2(a)'s promise of a prorated Payment on a \"Company initiated severance.\" A court could be asked whether every Company termination triggers proration.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Company initiated severance\" is undefined and it is genuinely unclear whether it covers any Company-initiated termination (including for cause or performance) or only a formal severance programme. That single phrase decides whether a departing employee is paid a prorated amount or nothing.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.55},
     {"issue": "The mandatory proration here is contradicted by Section 2(c), which begins \"Notwithstanding any provision hereof to the contrary\" and forfeits the whole Payment on a Company conclusion of poor performance, and by Section 4's unlimited termination right. Which controls a Company-initiated exit is not resolved by the text.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.40}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The carve-out for \"retirement in connection with a Company initiated severance\" repeats an undefined term and gives no test for the required connection. Whether a pressured or constructive resignation counts as \"your resignation\" is likewise left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38},
     {"issue": "Total forfeiture on resignation is hard to square with Section 2(a), which prorates the Payment for time actually served, and with Section 12's bar on diminishing accrued rights. A court could be asked whether any portion had already vested.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Forfeiture turns on the Company \"conclud[ing]\" that \"you failed to meet performance expectations,\" a standard with no defined content and no stated process, and the closing trigger sweeps in any violation of the Code of Conduct \"in any respect.\" Whether these are unreviewable Company judgments or objective conditions is the obvious battleground.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.52},
     {"issue": "The \"Notwithstanding any provision hereof to the contrary\" opener collides with Section 2(a)'s mandatory proration on a Company initiated severance and with Section 12's promise that nothing shall diminish accrued rights. Which of the competing overriding clauses prevails is not settled by the instrument.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The proviso applies only \"in the case of certain leaves of absences\" without identifying which, and makes early payment merely permissive (\"may be paid\"). The class of qualifying leaves cannot be determined from the text.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.09}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The transfer bar runs \"until the Agreement has expired,\" which is odd where the Payment is due 15 business days after the Retention Period ends and the Agreement may be satisfied earlier or terminated. The end point of the restriction is imprecise.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.09}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.12},
     {"issue": "The unlimited right to terminate employment \"with or without cause\" sits against Section 2(a), which promises a prorated Payment when employment ends by Company initiated severance, and Section 2(c), which forfeits it. A court could be asked how a no-cause Company termination is classified.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]}
]}

